\section{Balancing functions}{
\code{balanceRDD()} looks for a function called \verb{balance_<RDD>()}, where
\verb{<RDD>} is the name of the density-dependence function of the model, the
name you give to \code{setReproduction(params, RDD = ...)} or to
\code{setRateFunction(params, "RDD", ...)}. It is looked up in the same way as
the density-dependence function itself, so a balancing function that is
defined next to its density-dependence function is found.

Mizer provides balancing functions for its own density-dependence
functions. By default they keep \code{erepro} and change the parameter of the
density dependence:
\itemize{
\item \code{balance_BevertonHoltRDD()} changes \code{R_max}. It also accepts one of the
arguments \code{erepro}, \code{R_max} or \code{reproduction_level}, which choose the
Beverton-Holt curve as described in \code{\link[=setBevertonHolt]{setBevertonHolt()}}.
\item \code{balance_RickerRDD()} changes \code{ricker_b}. It also accepts \code{erepro}.
\item \code{balance_SheperdRDD()} changes \code{sheperd_b} and keeps \code{sheperd_c}. It also
accepts \code{erepro}.
\item \code{balance_constantRDD()} changes \code{constant_reproduction}.
\item \code{balance_noRDD()} changes \code{erepro}, because \code{\link[=noRDD]{noRDD()}} has no parameter of
its own.
}

When \code{erepro} cannot be kept because the density-independent reproduction
rate is smaller than the required rate, \code{erepro} is increased to the
smallest value that works, with a warning.
}

\section{Density-dependence functions without a balancing function}{
If there is no balancing function, mizer keeps the parameters of the
density dependence and solves numerically for the values of \code{erepro} that
give the required reproduction rate, and says that it has done so. This
works for any density dependence in which the reproduction rate of a species
responds to the egg production, including density dependence that couples
several species. Only \code{erepro} can be adjusted in this way, so
arguments in \code{...} are then not allowed.

If no value of \code{erepro} gives the required rate, for example because the
density dependence ignores the egg production, the parameters are left
unchanged with a warning. The same happens when the density dependence
depends on time, which mizer checks by evaluating it at a few different
times: such a model has no steady state to balance it to.
}

\section{Writing a balancing function}{
To control how your own density-dependence function \code{myRDD()} is balanced,
write a function \code{balance_myRDD()} with the arguments \code{params}, \code{rdi} and
\code{rdd_target}, followed by any arguments that choose which parameters are
changed. It should return \code{params} with the parameters changed so that
\code{myRDD()} gives \code{rdd_target} when the density-independent reproduction rate
is \code{rdi}, scaled by any change you make to \code{erepro}. It must leave alone the
species for which \code{rdd_target} is \code{NA}. It may assume that the
density-independent reproduction rate is proportional to \code{erepro}, as it is
with \code{\link[=mizerRDI]{mizerRDI()}}.
}

\section{Extension packages}{
An extension package that changes the reproduction rate through a
\code{\link[=projectRDD]{projectRDD()}} method should also provide a \code{balanceRDD()} method. That
method undoes the change to the target and passes it on to the next method,
which in the end balances the density-dependence function named in the
model. For an extension that adds immigration to the recruitment:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{projectRDD.myExtension <- function(params, rdi, ...) \{
    NextMethod() + other_params(params)$immigration
\}
balanceRDD.myExtension <- function(params, rdi = getRDI(params),
                                   rdd_target = getRequiredRDD(params),
                                   reproduction_level = NULL, ...) \{
    immigration <- other_params(params)$immigration
    if (!is.null(reproduction_level)) \{
        reproduction_level <- 1 - (1 - reproduction_level) *
            rdd_target / (rdd_target - immigration)
    \}
    NextMethod(rdd_target = rdd_target - immigration,
               reproduction_level = reproduction_level)
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A \code{reproduction_level} is the reproduction level of the whole reproduction
rate, as given by \code{\link[=reproduction_level]{reproduction_level()}}, so a method that passes one on
has to convert it to the level of the part it passes it to, as above.

Without a \code{balanceRDD()} method, the balancing function of the named
density-dependence function would not know about the change, so mizer
instead solves numerically for \code{erepro}, as described above, through the
whole of \code{\link[=projectRDD]{projectRDD()}}.
}

